From 7309726a239615be91189a29928888128adda576 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 16:03:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] A varp is an int unless it says type=boolean, and rule 24 is
 that, checked

FIXES THE BUILD, AGAIN, IN THE SAME FILE. %tan_window_canifis was declared with no
type=, which makes it an int, and the script reads and writes it as a boolean:

    ERROR: Type mismatch: 'boolean' was given but 'int' was expected.   (x2)
    ERROR: Operator '=' cannot be applied to 'int', 'boolean'.

Three errors in one file, and the compiler points at the script, where nothing is
wrong. The declaration is what was missing. bees.varp and quest_fishingcompo.varp are
the two files in this repo that got it right, and neither was read first - the usual
story.

rs2check RULE 24 is that error moved earlier: a varp compared or assigned against the
literals true or false whose block does not declare type=boolean. It fires on exactly
the three lines the build named and on nothing else in 1714 scripts, and --selftest
covers it, so all 23 rules go red in the right place.

This one is worth having where the pointer lint was not. It asks one question with one
answer, it has no false positives, and its entire value is that the cloud sandbox
cannot run a build - the compiler already catches this in a second. A rule that
duplicates the compiler is worth shipping when you cannot run the compiler; a rule that
GUESSES at what the compiler does, which is what the active_npc lint was doing with its
nine false positives, is not.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
